1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,480
 Here we are fifteen years later and I still wouldn't change a thing.

2
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:15,120
 This week a podcast listener asked me if I've changed my opinion on my top three exercises

3
00:00:15,120 --> 00:00:17,100
 for increasing the vertical jump.

4
00:00:17,100 --> 00:00:21,160
 And after thinking about it, no, I wouldn't change a thing.

5
00:00:21,160 --> 00:00:25,400
 Most of you have probably never seen these exercises before, but they're not new.

6
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:30,520
 All of these clips are from 2011, yet I still think these are three of the most effective

7
00:00:30,520 --> 00:00:34,140
 special strength exercises for improving the vertical jump.

8
00:00:34,140 --> 00:00:38,640
 This first exercise is referred to as a powerbomb kettlebell swing.

9
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:43,620
 It's still one of my favorite ways to develop violent hip extension due to the accelerated

10
00:00:43,620 --> 00:00:45,440
 eccentric portion of the lift.

11
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:50,560
 Basically, a training partner throws the kettlebell down, which leads to a rapid descent.

12
00:00:50,560 --> 00:00:55,440
 And based on Newton's third law, we know that for every action there is an equal and

13
00:00:55,440 --> 00:00:57,080
 opposite reaction.

14
00:00:57,080 --> 00:01:01,280
 So the rapid descent leads to fast, powerful hip extension.

15
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:05,540
 Next is a depth jump into an underhand med ball throw for height.

16
00:01:05,540 --> 00:01:10,600
 This exercise also accelerates the eccentric while teaching the athlete to absorb force

17
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:16,000
 and immediately redirect it explosively, which is exactly what great jumpers do.

18
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:21,240
 And finally, we have the dumbbell drop box jump, which is a phenomenal reactive power

19
00:01:21,240 --> 00:01:26,000
 exercise that teaches athletes to produce force quickly, all while minimizing ground

20
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:27,120
 contact time.

21
00:01:27,120 --> 00:01:32,040
 It's basically a super set of a vertical jump holding dumbbells right into an unloaded box

22
00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:33,040
 jump.

23
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:37,260
 So the loaded vertical jump potentiates the unloaded box jump, allowing you to produce

24
00:01:37,260 --> 00:01:39,160
 more force and jump higher.

25
00:01:39,160 --> 00:01:45,000
 So there you have it, three advanced exercises that have absolutely stood the test of time.

26
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:47,200
 Hopefully you found this content informative.

27
00:01:47,200 --> 00:01:51,360
 And if you did follow me, I've been coaching for 30 years and got a lot of cool shit to

28
00:01:51,360 --> 00:01:52,360
 share.

29
00:01:52,360 --> 00:01:52,960
 Here we are.

